------------------------------- Form Value Control --------------------------------------------------

- CREATE A CUSTOM BIRTH YEAR INPUT COMPONENT WITH FORMVALUECONTROL.

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite "Signal Forms" chapter

- 💡To help our users, we’d like to let them input just the last two digits of their birth year
    (for example, "84" for 1984, "12" for 2012, etc.). Is it a great idea? Probably not, but it’s
    a good exercise to learn how to create a custom form component with signal forms.
    

1️⃣ Generate a new component called birth-year-input with the Angular CLI.
        
        ng generate component birth-year-input
    
    Use the following template for the component:

        <div class="row d-flex align-items-center">
            <div class="col-3">
                <input class="form-control" type="number" />
            </div>
            <div class="col formatted-year">
                <!-- formatted year here -->
            </div>
        </div>

    This template has an input field and a div to display the formatted year.


2️⃣ For the TypeScript part, implement the FormValueControl<number | null> interface from
    @angular/forms/signals. The component must also have:
    👉 a required inputId input signal to set the input field id;
    👉 a required value model of type number | null, which is the two-way binding with the signal
        form;
    👉 a required touched input of type boolean, which receives the touched state from the signal
        form;
    👉 a touch output of type void, used to notify the signal form when the user touches the field;
    👉 a required disabled input of type boolean, which receives the disabled state from the signal
        form;
    👉 a required invalid input of type boolean, which receives the invalid state from the signal
        form;
    👉 a rawValue field using transformedValue.

    The rawValue field should use the value model as its source. Its parse function must turn null
    or NaN into { value: null }, and otherwise return { value: this.computeYear(raw) }. Its format
    function must return value ?? null.

        readonly inputId = input.required<string>();
        readonly value = model.required<number | null>();
        readonly touched = input.required<boolean>();
        readonly touch = output<void>();
        readonly disabled = input.required<boolean>();
        readonly invalid = input.required<boolean>();
        protected readonly rawValue = transformedValue(this.value, {
            parse: (raw: number | null) => {
                return { value: raw === null || isNaN(raw) ? null : this.computeYear(raw) };
            },
            format: (value: number | null) => value ?? null
        });


3️⃣ Add the computation of the year in a new private method called computeYear. This method should
    return the formatted year, using the following rules:
    👉 if the value is negative or greater than 100, return the value;
    👉 else if the value is greater than the last two digits of the current year, return
        (firstTwoDigitsOfTheCurrentYear - 1) * 100 + value (for example, if the user inputs 82,
        the year should be 1982);
    👉 otherwise, return firstTwoDigitsOfTheCurrentYear * 100 + value (for example, if the user
        inputs 10, the year should be 2010).
    
    To get the first two digits and last two digits of the current year, you can use the following
    code:

        const lastTwoDigitsOfTheCurrentYear = new Date().getFullYear() % 100;
        const firstTwoDigitsOfTheCurrentYear = Math.floor(new Date().getFullYear() / 100);


4️⃣ Add an onBirthYearInput method that will be called on input events. This method should get the
    native input value with:

        const value = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).valueAsNumber;
    
    Then set rawValue with this value. transformedValue will update the form value automatically.


5️⃣ In the template:
    👉 use the inputId signal to set the input field ID;
    👉 bind the native input value to rawValue();
    👉 bind the native input disabled state to disabled();
    👉 add a class binding to apply the Bootstrap is-invalid class when the field is both touched
        and invalid;
    👉 listen to the input event and call onBirthYearInput($event);
    👉 listen to the blur event and emit the touch output with touch.emit();
    👉 display value() in the formatted year column.

        <input class="form-control"
        [id]="inputId()"
        [value]="rawValue()"
        [disabled]="disabled()"
        [class.is-invalid]="touched() && invalid()"
        (input)="onBirthYearInput($event)"
        (blur)="touch.emit()"
        type="number" />


6️⃣ You can now use the new component in the register form. Import BirthYearInput in the Register
    component, add it to the component imports, and replace the birth year native input with:

        <pr-birth-year-input inputId="birth-year" [formField]="userForm.birthYear" />
    
    Everything should work as before, with the bonus that the user can now type a two-digit year
    and the birth year input gets the red border when invalid.


