😮🧠The confusion is coming from mixing up HTML attributes with Angular inputs. They are completely
different things.

✅ [id]="inputId()" : property binding
    Here, id is the HTML id attribute of the <input> element. and we are binding id attribute
    dynamically with our input signal property inputId().
    🔥 So when we use birth-year-input component in the parent component like:

        <pr-birth-year-input inputId="birth-year" />  
        or,
        <pr-birth-year-input [inputId]="'birth-year'" />
    
    <input class="form-control" [id]="inputId()" ... /> becomes <input id="birth-year">

⁉️ Why not just write <input id="birth-year">?
    Because our component should be reusable. Imagine you have two instances:

        <pr-birth-year-input inputId="father-birth-year"></pr-birth-year-input>
        <pr-birth-year-input inputId="mother-birth-year"></pr-birth-year-input>
    
    If we hardcoded <input id="birth-year">, both inputs have the same ID, which is invalid HTML and
    breaks labels and accessibility.

✅ (blur)="touch.emit()" : event binding
    Here, blur is a standard HTML DOM event. It fires when an element loses focus. for example when
    user types something inside the input and click somewhere else, the blur event fires.
    So we are binding this blur event with the output property touch. It means "I'm notifying whoever
    is using this component that the field has been touched."
    This is needed for displaying validation error message. 

